  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Very warm and muggy airmass continues to plague\nmuch of the Carolinas and southeastern CONUS this afternoon as \na frontal boundary remains stalled from the Piedmont into \nsoutheastern VA. 1000-850 MB thicknesses between 1430-1435 m \npoint to highs well into the 90s today, and are expected to be a\nfew meters higher tomorrow. Lingering cloud cover from upstream\nconvection could temper full potential insolation, but combined\nwith oppressive Tds expected to jump into the mid to \npotentially upper 70s, this will result in conditions feeling \nlike 105-110 degrees across almost all of eastern NC. NBM shows \na modest signal for WBGT values of 90 (considered very high) \nacross our southwestern counties south of Highway 70, and it is \nhere where a potential overnight upgrade to Extreme Heat Warning\nmay be considered. For now, however, after collaboration with \nneighboring offices and considering uncertainty of convective \ndebris cloud cover, have settled for an area-wide Heat Advisory.\nWe continue to encourage anyone planning on spending time \noutdoors to stay hydrated and to limit exposure to the heat, if \nable.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Outside of a spotty shower or thunderstorm \nfocused along sea and sound breeze, today will be dry for the \nregion. Latest 12z guidance still depicts a small cluster of \nshowers and storms nearing the northern zones tonight, but with \nwaning instability and poor upper-level support odds do not \nfavor this holding together into our FA. Therefore, dropped PoP \nmentions overnight.\n\nAttention turns to tomorrow as aforementioned frontal boundary \nsags southward and mid-level shortwave, currently associated \nwith remarkably persistent MCS over the Great Lakes, quickly \npushes towards the mid-Atlantic by mid-day tomorrow on the \nperiphery of a persistent sub-tropical ridge centered over the \nsoutheastern CONUS. This should provide more support for \nscattered shower and thunderstorms, although some questions \nremain on available lifting mechanisms. Most hi-res guidance \npoints to at least sea and sound breezes doing their usual work,\nbut there is more disagreement on activity approaching from \nnorth to south likely stemming from potential MCV progression \nfrom expected convective activity across the Midwest this \nafternoon and tonight. Temperance of instability owing to debris\nfrom overnight convection is also a factor. For now, high-end \nchance PoPs are warranted.\n\nPre-storm environment tomorrow is marginally conducive for \nstronger to severe storms as well as isolated flooding \npotential, supported by both NWP and AI guidance. More favorable\nenvironments are displaced farther north and west with \neffective shear of around 20 kt and MLCAPEs of around 2000 J/kg,\nalong with PWATs around 2 inches. Activity should be mainly \ndiurnal, although there is a risk of some overnight convection \nas upstream activity migrates along the sub-tropical ridge.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...A second frontal boundary looks to approach \nearly next week after a brief lull over the weekend. Still \nplenty of details to hammer out over 5 days out, but medium- \nrange AI NWP guidance points once again to at least a marginal \nrisk of stronger storms for portions of the Carolinas, along \nwith a heavy rainfall risk as humid airmass remains unyielding. \nReturn of heat early next week also points to another period of \npotential heat headlines.",
